body:
> Thanks for spotting this, I created a PR to do so here: [#12283](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12283)

Thanks for putting this up!


> > The (draft) menu elements spec PR has a [definition just like this](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12011/changes#diff-41cf6794ba4200b839c53531555f0f3998df4cbb01a4d5cb0b94e3ca5e23947dR90895-R90897) - I wonder if we could separate that logic out and land it sooner, to help out here?
> 
> This is a different thing which makes menu elements behave as popovers without the element actually having the popover attribute. In this case, the picker element really does have the popover=auto attribute.

I agree - if the spec explicitly says to use `popover` then this isn't needed.

> > Chromium similarly [uses the `popover` attribute](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/forms/select_type.cc;l=602;drc=1c29e9f7622dd2a8b818c0991ea530960aea2ff4) in a UA shadow root, and we have [similar workarounds](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/resources/html.css;l=1848;drc=a4ce098da63ec8e28b5bfe03273e403681edfebe).
> 
> I'm not sure why we're doing `[popover]:where(:not(:popover-open))`, but I don't think that its for customizable select.

It's mentioned in the comment there, but it's so these two rules don't fight, in the case of `<dialog popover>` which is open as a modal dialog. Without `:where()`, the dialog would stay `display:none` because the top rule would have higher specificity:

```
dialog:not([open]), [popover]:where(:not(:popover-open)) {
    display: none;
}
dialog[open], [popover]:popover-open {
    display: block;
}
```
